{"title":"2025 USAMO Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be positive integers with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/9/0597f7445e43607df112da8796f2f23d43940c53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\geq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" />. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> cupcakes of different flavors arranged around a circle and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> people who like cupcakes. Each person assigns a nonnegative real number score to each cupcake, depending on how much they like the cupcake. Suppose that for each person <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, it is possible to partition the circle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> cupcakes into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> groups of consecutive cupcakes so that the sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />'s scores of the cupcakes in each group is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Prove that it is possible to distribute the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> cupcakes to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> people so that each person <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> receives cupcakes of total score at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Arbitrarily pick any one person — call her Pip — and her <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> arcs. The initial idea is to try to apply Hall’s marriage lemma to match the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> people with Pip’s arcs (such that each such person is happy with their matched arc). To that end, construct the obvious bipartite graph <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a67bc43356a23eeb658084101683a2dbd7a8f69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{G}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"16\" /> between the people and the arcs for Pip. We now consider the following algorithm, which takes several steps.  \n</p>\n<ul><li>If a perfect matching of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a67bc43356a23eeb658084101683a2dbd7a8f69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{G}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"16\" /> exists, we’re done!</li></ul>\n<ul><li>We’re probably not that lucky. Per Hall’s condition, this means there is a <i>bad</i> set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/c/00c074178a2b8080056ecff4cee7ea1ebe1d8f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> of people, who are compatible with fewer than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/c/82c4ab584efc4a0b31f62e2c6242c55745548895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\mathcal{B}_1|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"31\" height=\"20\" /> of the arcs. Then delete <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/c/00c074178a2b8080056ecff4cee7ea1ebe1d8f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> and the neighbors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/c/00c074178a2b8080056ecff4cee7ea1ebe1d8f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" />, then try to find a matching on the remaining graph.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>If a matching exists now, terminate the algorithm. Otherwise, that means there’s another <i>bad</i> set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f21d42106c53c858e7abc39933a026f0e4faf43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> for the remaining graph. We again delete <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f21d42106c53c858e7abc39933a026f0e4faf43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" />and the fewer than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f21d42106c53c858e7abc39933a026f0e4faf43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> neighbors.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Repeat until some perfect matching <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> is possible in the remaining graph, i.e. there are no more bad sets (and then terminate once that occurs).</li></ul>\n<p>Since Pip is a universal vertex, it’s impossible to delete Pip, so the algorithm does indeed terminate with nonempty <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>A cartoon of this picture is shown below.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2025_USAMO_PROBLEM_6.jpeg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/28/2025_USAMO_PROBLEM_6.jpeg/550px-2025_USAMO_PROBLEM_6.jpeg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"550\" height=\"497\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/2/28/2025_USAMO_PROBLEM_6.jpeg 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>We commit to assigning each of person in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> their matched arc (in particular if there are no bad sets at all, the problem is already solved). Now we finish the problem by induction on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> (for the remaining people) by simply deleting the arcs used up by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" />. To see why this deletion-induction works, consider any particular person Quinn not in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" />. By definition, Quinn is not happy with any of the arcs in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" />. So when an arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/7/0b7bf42db84e4d2dbb7bf55476c12bf9f6233bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> is deleted, it had value less than 1 for Quinn so in particular it couldn’t contain entirely any of Quinn’s arcs. Hence at most one endpoint among Quinn’s arcs was in the deleted arc A. When this happens, this causes two arcs of Quinn to merge, and the merged value is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/a/0eaa38ca21d6caae177979580a700a97ff83d304.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(\\geq 1) + (\\geq 1) - (\\leq 1) \\geq 1\\]\" width=\"202\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>meaning the induction is OK. See below for a cartoon of the deletion, with Pip's arcs in blue and Quinn's in red, where here, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Usamoq1p2evanchen.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9f/Usamoq1p2evanchen.png/550px-Usamoq1p2evanchen.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"550\" height=\"225\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/9/9f/Usamoq1p2evanchen.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p><b>Remark.</b> This deletion argument can be thought of in some special cases even before the realization of Hall, in the case where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/d/e6d64e475f6183ad1b0adadca7cbfa6a14ed5d75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> has only one person (Pip). This amounts to saying that if one of Pip’s arcs isn’t liked by anybody, then that arc can be deleted and the induction carries through.\n</p><p><b>Remark.</b> Conversely, it should be reasonable to expect Hall’s theorem to be helpful even before finding the deletion argument. While working on this problem, one of the first things I said was: “We should let Hall do the heavy lifting for us: find a way to make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> groups that satisfy Hall’s condition, rather than an assignment of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> groups to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> people.” As a general heuristic, for any type of “compatible matching” problem, Hall’s condition is usually the go-to tool. (It is much easier to verify Hall’s condition than actually find the matching yourself.) Actually in most competition problems, if one realizes one is in a Hall setting, one is usually close to finishing the problem. This is a relatively rare example in which one needs an additional idea to go alongside Hall’s theorem.\n</p><p>~Evan Chen (From USAMO 2025 Solution Notes, found <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://web.evanchen.cc/exams/USAMO-2025-notes.pdf\">here</a>)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If the jury scores a cupcake <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/4/6740b7bef0abfc8864fef0f84f0cb186ccf147dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> for all cupcakes then for all the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> cupcakes receiving score <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/f/72fd050c30ca6af66865c590f8dbf22bbf005bcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\in (1, \\infty)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"84\" height=\"20\" /> we prove the problem for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/2/26295c02e75367edf1767cd6afb864b36c114f30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> cupcake lovers and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/9/a09ffb76e02a36a83c22d4794ba8653bfd3613ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m-c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> cupcakes. Hence, consider scores in the range <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/1/101678300d1aa11009468da8419aaf2c57b795bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Suppose the cupcakes are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/a/77a44f0545c2215b46db41ec9d8bc6ded7b341fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m_1, m_2, \\dots, m_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"126\" height=\"13\" /> flavored and the people <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/6/a86447bf6d168fb6ec6b55bb0aa261bbc74fed3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n_1, n_2, \\dots, n_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"13\" /> are to rate each. \n</p><p><b>Lemma 1:</b> The score is always <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/9/d099d5d85bbefa87c33db3d9048b62db6d6d69eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S \\geq \\frac{n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"58\" height=\"34\" />.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 1:</b>\n</p><p>If the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />th cupcake is scored by the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3eabe6c97f03e413d1aa3c292e9478f286e64df1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$o$\" width=\"9\" height=\"8\" />th jury, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/4/0748cd16096f04814c4f1ff5fb07f0fe4f777234.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_{k,o}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> is the given score. Then,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/5/0855e1b2dc1ee791764a0408c86962fb13399229.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S=\\sum_{k=1}^m s_{k,P} \\geq n\\]\" width=\"130\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>by the problem statement. Taking the average by dividing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/5/4659ee9ed51157f75502b0ceabc4b396eccd39ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"38\" /> yields the result, as in general the sum of each value of individual scores that are not that average, when constructing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> groups of the same modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> cupcake, is always greater than the average. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2:</b> USAMO P6 Problem Statement\n</p><p><b>Proof of the Lemma 2:</b>\n</p><p>Rearrangement Inequality is a great starter here.\n</p><p>Assume the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />th cupcake is maximally liked by the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />th contender in moduli order (modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> here). Then,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/9/3e97f594dd09ac1df15bc37789dee1f3f1dd6cea.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Score Cupcake 1} : s_{1,n} \\leq s_{1,n-1} \\leq \\dots \\leq s_{1,1}\\]\" width=\"326\" height=\"17\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/1/691fb78f85ceac5f345129a6ae1f41172172a37a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Score Cupcake 2}: s_{2,n} \\leq \\dots \\leq s_{2,1} \\leq s_{2,2}\\]\" width=\"309\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/7/16762e47f22f53e2431935f211fa82570b46b6cb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dots\\]\" width=\"18\" height=\"2\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/2/00249f73b34362587d8359819d22736c043f440e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Score Cupcake} \\ n: s_{n,1} \\leq \\dots \\leq s_{n,n}\\]\" width=\"264\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/a/02ac7f5ac8ea78bf5a5da15bfeb29ce1fbc9e598.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Score Cupcake} \\ n+1: s_{n+1,n} \\leq \\dots \\leq s_{n+1,1}\\]\" width=\"328\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/7/16762e47f22f53e2431935f211fa82570b46b6cb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dots\\]\" width=\"18\" height=\"2\" />\n</p><p>By <b>Lemma 1</b>, we can pair each of the cupcakes in respective modular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, so for example, Cupcake 1 is paired with Cupcake <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />, Cupcake 2 is paired with Cupcake <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/6/1f6e3acd481bacb78f324773254902e3a08d848f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />, ..., Cupcake <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/a/c2ae7e65cc3bab56608f8312f9535d0fb0f910e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nk+m \\ : \\ k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"142\" height=\"18\" /> is paired with Cupcake <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />. By Pigeonhole Principle it is certain the compiled scores of each cupcake must exceed or equal 1. All the remaining cupcakes can form the remainder of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> groups. Now, say that after obtaining their groups, the said jury \"leave\" the junction. All of those left with one cupcake form a new compendium. We prove the following sub-lemma:\n</p><p><b>Lemma 3:</b> These people must be the ones who like their own cupcake!\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 3:</b>\n</p><p>It's obvious! By the way we originally paired each person, (which is the minimum by Rearrangement Inequality), it is noticed that the remaining people have their liked cupcake! <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 2 Continued:</b>\n</p><p>A \"likeable score\", defined in <b>Lemma 1</b> must be any score <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/2/5324b6d8f94b38b6c8a371906ee07cd1c31fbdc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S \\in (\\frac{n}{m}, 1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"85\" height=\"34\" />. Indeed, if each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> people rate this score, the remaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/8/7a81152e3264a5b1cca111d8db3ca447672bb538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m-nk, nk \\leq m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"130\" height=\"18\" /> people score the cupcake greater-than or equal to the average, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b1137b1980a40188b1ace4dbaddb02e5092334b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m-n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"37\" /> by <b>Lemma 1</b>. But, this forces <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/4/6740b7bef0abfc8864fef0f84f0cb186ccf147dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" />, hence the Lemma is complete. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><b>2025 USAMO/P6:</b>\n</p><p>By <b>Lemma 2</b>, there will always exist a configuration of cupcakes and people who love cupcakes who can score and partition given the constraint held true in <b>Lemma 1</b> such that they each have a cupcake scored greater than or equal to 1. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO\" title=\"2025 USAMO\">2025 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2025 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2025\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2025 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Problem</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2025 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2025 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2025 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2025 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2025 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017051734\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.054 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.075 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 451/1000000\nPos